    "body": "DoorDash has launched a retail returns service that lets customers pay $7.99 for a driver to pick up an eligible purchase at their door and take it back to the store. Dasher Returns is available in select cities, with a nationwide rollout expected in November.\nWhat’s Related\nThe company announced the service Sept. 30 at its Dash Forward event. Customers do not need a box or shipping label, and DoorDash says pickup can happen in as little as 30 minutes. The retailer processes the return when the driver brings the item back, with the customer receiving a refund within days.\nDoorDash also unveiled DoorDash Air, a drone delivery system that will begin pilot deliveries in Northern California. The returns service is already operating, while the drone announcement outlines an upcoming test with restaurant partners.\nRELATED STORIES: DoorDash Spending $50 Million to Help Drivers With Gas Costs | DoorDash Wins FAA Approval to Launch Its Own Drone Delivery Service\nHow the returns service works\nDasher Returns covers eligible retail orders and handles the trip from the customer’s home back to the store. DoorDash says it processes returns on the spot, allowing the item to go directly back on the shelf.\nThe service’s advertised speed comes with conditions. DoorDash says pickup times vary and are not guaranteed, refund timing can differ, and the $7.99 price is subject to change.\nThe retail announcement also details the scale of its new shopping partnerships. Macy’s products will be available through more than 350 stores, while Anthropologie will be available at more than 220 locations. The North Face, Vans, and Timberland together bring more than 350 stores.\nDrone pilot starts with restaurant orders\nDoorDash Air’s first partners include Chipotle, Popeyes, and Momo N Curry. DoorDash did not provide a specific start date for the Northern California pilot.\nThe company says it designed the system around actual order weights, sizes, and delivery distances. Its work includes packaging , loading equipment, and the handoff from restaurant kitchens to aircraft.\nAccording to DoorDash, about 80% of typical restaurant orders are small and light enough for its aircraft to carry. Weather, flight range, and other conditions further narrow which orders can travel by air .\nDoorDash says initial tests produced average restaurant-to-door delivery times of under five minutes, based on a limited sample. Its delivery software will consider the order’s contents, weight, distance, and traffic when choosing between a driver, a ground robot , or a drone.",
